# Copy this to .env (or .env.local) for `next dev` and `npm run build` to
# pick it up. Next.js loads both automatically; nothing here reads it in any
# other way. The live build's values go in Netlify's own environment
# variables (Site configuration → Environment variables) — never here, and
# never in `netlify.toml`, which is committed.
#
# Absence is the default and the safe state: with neither variable set, the
# build has no loader script and no banner unit at all, which is every
# contributor's machine and every pull request this repository's own CI runs
# (`.github/workflows/verify.yml` sets fixture values for its own build, so
# the ad-rendering mechanics are still exercised there without needing the
# real account's secrets in a public workflow file).

# ==============================================================================
# Advertising — optional, and a client id and a banner slot go together.
# These are the same three names `src/server/config.ts` reads in the
# application repository: this is the same account's same banner unit,
# running on both origins, so one parser two deployments agree with is worth
# more than a second copy that can only drift from it.
# ==============================================================================

# The AdSense publisher id, ca-pub- followed by sixteen digits. The AdSense
# dashboard shows it as pub-...; the missing ca- prefix is refused at build
# time rather than failing silently by rendering nothing. This is not a
# secret — it is published in public/ads.txt and in every page's rendered
# HTML already — but it still belongs here rather than hardcoded, so this
# file and the application's point at the same account without a second
# place to edit.
# ADSENSE_CLIENT_ID=ca-pub-0000000000000000

# The one banner unit this site shows, on every page except the legal pages
# and the 404. Ten digits. Set together with ADSENSE_CLIENT_ID or not at all
# — setting one without the other refuses the build rather than showing
# nothing and failing silently.
# ADSENSE_BANNER_SLOT_ID=0000000000

# Whether Google's consent message decides if an ad may be personalized.
# Off, every request carries `requestNonPersonalizedAds`; on, it does not, and
# the message published in AdSense's own Privacy and messaging decides by
# region. The live site has it on, because the account publishes a European
# and a US-state message, and the privacy policy says so: a build with it off
# fails `tests/adsense.test.ts` by name. The parser's own default is off, as
# the application's is. Nothing here can check that a message is published;
# `docs/adsense.md` §5 and the `legal-review` skill are where that is decided
# and kept true.
# ADSENSE_CONSENT_MANAGED=true
